 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training requires approximately 1 million iterations [S1]. Each iteration involves forward passes through two networks and sampling.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of instances $K$ due to the storage of latent codes $\mathbf{z}_k^{(s)}$ and $\mathbf{z}_k^{(c)}$ ($2 \times 32 \times K$ floats) plus the network weights.
-*   **Performance:** On the PhotoShapes dataset, the method matches the performance of training independent NeRF models for each instance [S1]. It scales well to large datasets, such as the 626 instances in PhotoShapes [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficiently models shape and appearance differences across instances; avoids training separate models; generalizes to unseen views.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires joint optimization of codes and network weights; performance depends on the quality of the latent code initialization and optimization.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training time is proportional to the number of iterations (average 1M) and the number of instances $K$ [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of instances $K$ due to the storage of latent codes $\mathbf{z}^{(s)}_k$ and $\mathbf{z}^{(c)}_k$ [S1].
+*   **Performance:** On the PhotoShapes dataset, the method matches the performance of training independent NeRF models for each instance [S1].
+*   **Scalability:** The method scales well to more training instances. When training with all 626 instances of the PhotoShape dataset, it achieves reconstruction quality comparable to independent models [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Accurately models shape and appearance differences across instances; generalizes to unseen views; supports editing [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires joint optimization of codes and network weights, which can be computationally intensive [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in "Editing Conditional Radiance Fields" [S1]. It builds on the original NeRF paper [S1] and extends it by incorporating instance-specific latent codes. The method was evaluated on the PhotoShapes and Aubry chairs datasets [S1]. It is used for tasks such as 3D reconstruction, view synthesis, and editing of 3D instances [S1].
+*   **Origin:** Proposed in "Editing Conditional Radiance Fields" (2021) [S1].
+*   **History:** Builds on the original NeRF paper [45] and extends it to handle multiple instances by introducing latent codes [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Used for editing 3D objects, generalizing across instances, and rendering multiple views of objects with varying shape and appearance [S1].
 
